fact WorkItem(id: "WI-743", description: some(value: "DESIGN + FEATURE: a finite closed ADT should BE a generator — the mode-(out) COMPLETION of WI-742's `domain(?x, T)` goal: when `?x` is UNBOUND and T is a FINITE closed ADT, ENUMERATE T's inhabitants instead of delaying, so an enum needs no hand-written domain facts. (Revised 2026-07-17 after the WI-742 design settled: open question (3) is now ANSWERED, (4) is now ANSWERED, and the ACCEPTANCE's \"infinite T is a loud error\" clause was WRONG and is REPLACED — see CORRECTIONS.)\n\nMOTIVATION (user, Socratic, 2026-07-17, from the map-colouring example): a colour is one of exactly three things, and 'sort Colour { entity red, entity green, entity blue }' ALREADY says so — the spec calls a sort-with-constructors a closed ADT whose 'inhabitants [are] enumerated' and 'All constructors are enumerated' (kernel-language.md:620, :1483). Yet to actually enumerate them you must ALSO hand-write a wrapper sort + facts ('sort Palette { entity palette(c: Colour) }' + 'fact palette(c: red())' x3). The user's question: why? The information is already in the sort.\n\nTODAY (each verified live by deleting it): (a) THE SORT DOES NOT GENERATE — drop the facts, keep 'rule colouring(?wa: Colour, ?nt: Colour) :- neq(?wa, ?nt)', and it raises 'relation_floundered': nothing binds ?wa, so the neq TEST cannot decide. A sort declaration is not callable; SLD resolves goals against facts/rules. (b) A RULE-SHAPED generator ('rule palette(?c) :- eq(?c, red())' x3, no wrapper sort) enumerates BUT yields 'Relation[(wa: ?_, nt: ?_)]' — untyped columns, because a rule subgoal constrains nothing (the same root gap as WI-741 / the WI-714 recursion fix / WI-079). (c) So the entity field 'c: Colour' is the ONLY thing that types the column — which is what makes the wrapper sort load-bearing today. NOTE (b) and (c) are what WI-742 fixes: once `?x: T` on a relational head is honored, the column types from the ANNOTATION and the entity field stops being the only channel. This ticket removes the remaining half — the FACTS.\n\nTHE INSIGHT (user): 'this means typed rule heads'. WI-582 already gives '?x: T' a MEANING and a desugaring — 'keep(?x: Summable, ?y) = ?x' MEANS 'keep(?x, ?y) = ?x :- conforms(typeof(?x), Summable)' (wi582_typed_rule_pattern_test.rs:9-17): the loader strips the annotation from the head (discrim-tree indexes it identically — carrier-neutral) and installs a per-variable bound by DeBruijn index; at fire apply_eq_rules reads the matched value's CARRIED type (WI-578 value_type_term) and checks conformance, SUSPENDING where the type is under-determined. So '?x: T' is a TEST (mode in) — and an unbound ?x IS under-determined, which is exactly why it suspends rather than generating.\n\nTHE PROPOSAL = the DUAL reading, mode-directed. WI-742 establishes `domain(?x, T)` with two modes — (in) conformance test, (out) DELAY-and-rotate. This ticket adds the THIRD arm: (out) AND T is a FINITE closed ADT → ENUMERATE T's inhabitants as a choice point, rather than delaying. 'conforms(typeof(?x), T)' read in mode (out) IS a generator — precisely a CLP(FD) 'domain'. Payoff: 'rule colouring(?wa: Colour, ?nt: Colour, ...) :- neq(?wa,?nt), ...' with NO domain facts and NO Palette wrapper, and typed columns from WI-742.\n\nFINITENESS IS THE PRECISE LIMIT: only an ALL-NULLARY sort has a finite known inhabitant set. 'entity colour(name: String)' is infinite (String is); 'sort List { nil, cons(head, tail) }' is recursively infinite. So the rule is 'a closed ADT all of whose variants are nullary is a finite domain' — a compile-time-decidable property. This fits the EXISTING finiteness machinery (FiniteCollection / WI-589 / library-003 Phase C: 'providing collect IS the finiteness guarantee'); note a finite enum CAN honor collect, unlike Relation (maybe-infinite, WI-714). A wider rule (all variants nullary OR all fields finite — a finite PRODUCT is finite) is a possible later generalization; start with all-nullary.\n\nCORRECTIONS (2026-07-17, from the WI-742 design session):\n  (3) ANSWERED — \"interaction with WI-582's [simp]/[unfold]-only restriction and with WI-742 (relational typed heads are currently SILENTLY DROPPED)\". They are NOT silently dropped: that premise was refuted (WI-742 PREMISE CORRECTION — the loader IS loud; `anthill run` mutes it, which is WI-744). And the restriction is NOT principled — it is enforcement coverage (`typed_pattern_bounds_hold` has ONE call site, resolve.rs:2991), while docs/design/constrained-term-substrate.md:310 says op-head vs non-op-head is \"not a typing distinction\". WI-742 lifts it for relational heads.\n  (4) ANSWERED — \"does the generator belong in the resolver, or as a loader-derived clause set?\" THE RESOLVER, as the (out)+finite arm of WI-742's mode-directed `domain` builtin. A loader-derived clause set (auto-generating the palette facts) was the alternative and is REJECTED: it would materialize N facts per sort at load whether or not anything enumerates, it needs a synthetic predicate name nothing cites, and it splits one notion (`?x: T`) across two mechanisms (a body goal for the test, a clause set for the generator) — whereas one mode-directed goal covers both, which is exactly what makes it a CLP domain rather than a codegen trick.\n  ACCEPTANCE FIX — the original's \"an infinite/non-nullary sort in that position is a LOUD error, never a silent non-generation\" is WRONG and is REPLACED. `rule f(?x: String) :- eq(?x, \"abe\")` MUST work: `String` is infinite, so `domain` DELAYS, `eq` binds `?x`, rotation re-asks, the check passes, one row. Making infinite-T a LOAD error would reject that legitimate rule. Infinite T is a DELAY, not an error; only a STILL-UNBOUND-at-the-end `?x` flounders, and that already routes loudly through WI-737 (`Error[RelationFloundered]`). So the loud-over-silent property is preserved by the FLOUNDER, not by a load-time rejection.\n\nREMAINING OPEN QUESTIONS: (1) SURFACE — implicit (a typed head param of a finite sort just generates) or explicit (a derived 'Colour.values' relation, citable by name via WI-714's bare-qualified Sort.rule machinery, written ':- Colour.values(?wa)')? Implicit is terser but magic — a rule silently changes from test to generator with the finiteness of a sort it names, and adding one non-nullary variant to Colour would silently turn a working generator into a delay/flounder (arguably fine: that IS a loud flounder, not a wrong answer). Explicit is discoverable and reuses 052 wholesale, and post-WI-742 the \"a rule subgoal would not type ?wa\" objection DISSOLVES — the head annotation types it. NOTE the two are not exclusive: `Colour.values` could be the derived relation AND the implicit reading could be defined as sugar for it. (2) ORDERING / MULTIPLICITY of the generated inhabitants — declaration order (WI-720 ctor order exists) and once each, presumably; state it, since a bag vs set difference is observable in the solution count.\n\nDEPENDS ON WI-742 (which now delivers the `domain` goal this extends; the dependency is unchanged but its content is now the restriction-lift, not the mis-diagnosed silent skip).\n\nACCEPTANCE (if adopted): map-colouring's Colour needs no Palette wrapper and no domain facts — 'rule colouring(?wa: Colour, ...) :- neq(..)' enumerates 6 definite colourings and types as Relation[(wa: Colour, ...)]; a FINITE closed ADT in a typed head param generates in mode (out), in DECLARATION order, once per inhabitant; an INFINITE / non-nullary sort in that position DELAYS (not a load error) and yields correctly when a later goal binds it ('rule f(?x: String) :- eq(?x, \"abe\")' yields one row), flounding loudly (WI-737) only if still unbound at the end; the mode-(in) conformance test from WI-742 is unchanged; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-742"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-743", author: "user", content: "PROPOSAL 060 sec 2.2 (2026-08-07, user-approved) SETTLES open question (1) with a third option subsuming both listed ones, and adds a semantic rule: mode (out) does NOT special-case finite ADTs in the resolver -- `domain(?x, T)` dispatches to a MEMBER RELATION T defines, named `domain` (user-chosen name, over value_of; the goal is that member read through the type, hence one name). DERIVED for an all-nullary closed ADT (the finiteness gate unchanged; declaration order, once each -- open question (2) resolved to that presumption), USER-DEFINABLE for any sort (the Palette wrapper is a hand-written `domain` the language gave no name; absorbed into the sort). The implicit typed-head reading and the explicit citable relation are ONE thing. NEW SEMANTIC RULE, user-confirmed: `domain` is DOMAIN-DEFINING, not a generator hint -- where T defines it BOTH modes read it (mode (in) checks conformance AND membership; mode (out) enumerates); a generate-from-subset / accept-anything-conforming split makes the relation answer depend on call mode and is REFUSED. Derived enum case: membership == conformance, no extra cost. Rule-local narrowing stays an ordinary body goal. A sort with NO `domain` keeps the delay/flounder ladder exactly (the String acceptance case unchanged). ABSTRACT T dispatches through the requirement channel (WI-1040, proposal 060 sec 3 anchor); concrete T pins at typing. No 058 sec 3.10 tension: values enumerate by ordinary SLD choice, instances are never chosen. Acceptance sketch unchanged otherwise; the colouring example in 060 sec 2.2 is the driver (uses the sec 2.1 parameter form).", at: "2026-08-07T09:57:29Z")
